 ],
 "literal": "Day after day, even as days pass, he does not come / At all times, even in the evening, he does not appear / Where is he, O friend? Here, is this / the season he spoke of? Many times, / calling out to its grey-backed mate to be happy, / in the blink of an eye, what has it become? On the top of the ñemai tree, / desiring flesh, a vulture will sit, / on the sky-high craggy mountain traversed by him.",
 "translation": "Day after day passes, yet he does not return. At no hour of the day or night does he appear. Where can he be, my friend? Is this indeed the season he promised to return? On the high branches of the ñemai tree, where a vulture sits waiting, hungering for flesh, a male bird repeatedly calls out to its grey-backed mate to share its joy. He who traversed those sky-high, craggy mountains has still not come back.",
 "modern": "தோழி! ஒவ்வொரு நாளும் கடந்து போகிறது, ஆனால் அவர் வரவில்லை. எந்த நேரத்திலும் அவர் வந்து தோன்றவில்லை. அவர் எங்கே இருக்கிறாரோ? அவர் திரும்பி வருவதாகச் சொல்லிச் சென்ற பருவம் இதுதானோ? வானளாவிய உயர்ந்த மலைகளைக் கடந்து சென்ற அவர் இன்னும் வரவில்லை. அங்கே, ஞெமை மரத்தின் உச்சியில், தசை உணவை விரும்பி ஒரு பருந்து அமர்ந்திருக்கும். அதே மரத்தில், ஆண் பறவை தன் சாம்பல் நிறப் பெண் பறவையை மகிழ்ச்சியடையச் செய்யப் பலமுறை கூவி அழைக்கும்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her close friend in deep distress. The hero had departed to earn wealth, traversing a harsh desert path, and promised to return by a specific season. Although that season has arrived, he is nowhere to be seen, and the heroine wonders where he might be. The poem belongs to the pālai (desert) landscape, which represents separation and longing. The image of the vulture waiting for flesh on the ñemai tree highlights the dangerous and desolate nature of the path he took. In contrast, the male bird calling out to its grey-backed mate to share its joy emphasizes the heroine's own painful isolation.",
